\begin{theorem}
Let $F:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}$. Then $F$ satisfies (C), (CA), (M) and (LI) if and only if there is $a\in[0,1]$ such that
\[ F(x)=a\max(x)+(1-a)\min(x)\qquad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^2 . \]
In that case $a=F(1,0)$, so $a$ is unique. None of the four axioms can be dropped: $2\max(x)$ satisfies (CA), (M) and (LI) but not (C); $5+\min(x)$ satisfies (C), (M) and (LI) but not (CA); $2\max(x)-\min(x)$ satisfies (C), (CA) and (LI) but not (M); $x_1$ satisfies (C), (CA) and (M) but not (LI).
\end{theorem}
\begin{remark}
Without (LI), the proof below shows that (C), (CA) and (M) hold exactly when $F(x)=\min(x)+a_1(x_1-x_2)^+ + a_2(x_2-x_1)^+$ for some $a_1,a_2\in[0,1]$, with $a_1=F(1,0)$ and $a_2=F(0,1)$.
\end{remark}
